analysisEventIndex": 395, "attemptCount": 1, "toolCallCount": 1, "decisionPreview": "seg = current_frame.segmentation", "localContext": {"sections": [{"label": "USER PROMPT", "content": "The code executed 8 actions in the previous sequence.\nExecuted actions: MOUSE(row=33, col=45), MOUSE(row=27, col=39), MOUSE(row=33, col=39), MOUSE(row=27, col=33), MOUSE(row=33, col=33), MOUSE(row=27, col=27), MOUSE(row=33, col=27), MOUSE(row=27, col=15).\nYou are still on the same level.\nCurrent state: step 308, level 2.\nValid actions right now: LEFT, RIGHT, MOUSE, ACTION7.\nOnly tool: `python`. It receives `current_frame`, `previous_frame`, `history`, `transitions`, `last_transition`, `valid_actions`, `last_action_result`, and `action(actions)`. It also provides `animation()` (compact diff history), `last_animation` (actual frames from the latest action call), `animation_history`, and `frame_stats` -- optional; prefer `animation()` when transient pixels indicate hidden signal.\nOnly letter-coded board views and lightweight metadata are exposed; raw numeric color IDs are not available.\nKeep tool output compact: use `current_frame.segmentation` as the primary view, and `current_frame.ascii` only for a small specific region; never print full boards.\nFor the most recent change, compare `previous_frame` to `current_frame`, or `last_transition.before_frame` to `last_transition.after_frame`; `history[-1].frame` is the current frame, not the previous one.\nUse Python to inspect the evidence, refine that world model from the newest history, and search or score candidate actions or short sequences against the current goal as you currently understand it.\nMaintain a compact working world model of what the current level seems to contain, what actions appear to do, what the goal seems to be, what is still uncertain, and what plan currently looks best.\nBelow you are provided with the current world model from the previous turn. The default behavior is to copy it and add or remove things based on the evidence that you gathered. BEFORE EXECUTING NEW ACTIONS YOU MUST ALWAYS GIVE THE REVISED VERSION OF THE WORLD MODEL.\nYou may call `action(actions)` more than once in one Python snippet if your search or control loop needs it, but stop immediately if a result reports `game_over`, `level_completed`, or `done`.\nend of world model. \nFocus on what changed most recently in `history`, update the target environment change if needed, and separate gameplay-object changes from HUD-only changes.\nWhen ready, call `action(actions)` from inside the `python` tool with the best valid action or ordered batch selected by your code. If your code has found a reliable short sequence, prefer batching it in one call.\nYou may call `action(actions)` more than once in one Python snippet if your search or control loop needs it.\nIf you include assistant text before a tool call, keep it short and use it to update the world model. Helpful optional prefixes are `World model:`, `Goal model:`, `Action model:`, `Recent findings:`, `Open questions:`, `Plan:`, and `Cross-level notes:`.\nWhen calling `python`, emit exactly the tool-call format shown elsewhere in this prompt for this model. Use only that format; do not add markdown fences, prose wrappers, or alternate tool-call syntax. Do not quote or place tool-call markup inside explanatory text; when you decide to call the tool, emit the tool call itself.\nIf you use MOUSE, include integer row and col arguments.\nEFFICIENCY BUDGET \u2014 your score on each level is (baseline_actions / your_actions)^2, so every wasted action costs you quadratically.\nLevel 2: you have used 265 actions; a strong score needs about 48 or fewer. You are 5.5x over the target.\nIf you are not steadily making progress toward the level goal, commit to your single best hypothesis and the shortest sequence that tests it \u2014 do not exhaustively scan rows/columns or enumerate every option. Test one idea, read the result, then decide.", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "THINKING", "content": "Excellent! 8 more greens clicked. Now only 3 greens remain, and they're clustered near the player (cols 15, 21, 27 area). Looking at the image, I can see:\n- 3 green blocks remaining, forming an L-shape near the player\n- The player is at the bottom\n- The purple blocks are still at the top\nThe remaining greens are close to the player. I need to be careful. Let me check their exact positions and click them, avoiding the ones directly above the player.", "kind": "reasoning", "inContext": false}, {"label": "TOOL CALL: python", "content": "<tool_call>\n<function=python>\n<parameter=code>\n# Check remaining greens\nseg = current_frame.segmentation\ngreens = [n for n in seg['nodes'] if n['color'] == 'N']\nplayer = [n for n in seg['nodes'] if n['color'] == 'b'][0]\np_cols = [p[1] for p in player['boundary']]\np_center_col = (min(p_cols) + max(p_cols)) // 2\nprint(f\"Player center col: {p_center_col}\")\nprint(f\"Remaining greens: {len(greens)}\")\n\nfor n in greens:\n    cols = [p[1] for p in n['boundary']]\n    rows = [p[0] for p in n['boundary']]\n    center_row = (min(rows) + max(rows)) // 2\n    center_col = (min(cols) + max(cols)) // 2\n    dist = abs(center_col - p_center_col)\n    print(f\"  Green at ({center_row}, {center_col}), dist={dist}\")\n\n# The greens near player are dangerous. Maybe I need to move the player first?\n# Let me check if moving LEFT or RIGHT helps avoid them\n# Player is at col ~20. Greens are at cols 15, 21, 27\n# If I move LEFT, player goes to ~14, then green at 15 is above\n# If I move RIGHT, player goes to ~26, then green at 27 is above\n# Hmm, tricky. Let me try clicking the ones that are NOT directly above first\nbatch = []\nfor n in greens:\n    cols = [p[1] for p in n['boundary']]\n    rows = [p[0] for p in n['boundary']]\n    center_row = (min(rows) + max(rows)) // 2\n    center_col = (min(cols) + max(cols)) // 2\n    dist = abs(center_col - p_center_col)\n    if dist > 1:  # Avoid directly above\n        batch.append({'action': 'MOUSE', 'row': center_row, 'col': center_col})\n\nprint(f\"Clicking {len(batch)} non-adjacent greens\")\naction(batch)\n</parameter>\n</function>\n</tool_call>", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "TOOL RESULT: python", "content": "Player center col: 20\nRemaining greens: 3\n  Green at (27, 21), dist=1\n  Green at (33, 15), dist=5\n  Green at (33, 21), dist=1\nClicking 1 non-adjacent greens", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}], "hasExactModelContext": false, "requestIndexWithinTurn": null, "messageCount": 0}, "context": {"sections": [{"label": "USER PROMPT", "content": "The code executed 8 actions in the previous sequence.\nExecuted actions: MOUSE(row=33, col=45), MOUSE(row=27, col=39), MOUSE(row=33, col=39), MOUSE(row=27, col=33), MOUSE(row=33, col=33), MOUSE(row=27, col=27), MOUSE(row=33, col=27), MOUSE(row=27, col=15).\nYou are still on the same level.\nCurrent state: step 308, level 2.\nValid actions right now: LEFT, RIGHT, MOUSE, ACTION7.\nOnly tool: `python`. It receives `current_frame`, `previous_frame`, `history`, `transitions`, `last_transition`, `valid_actions`, `last_action_result`, and `action(actions)`. It also provides `animation()` (compact diff history), `last_animation` (actual frames from the latest action call), `animation_history`, and `frame_stats` -- optional; prefer `animation()` when transient pixels indicate hidden signal.\nOnly letter-coded board views and lightweight metadata are exposed; raw numeric color IDs are not available.\nKeep tool output compact: use `current_frame.segmentation` as the primary view, and `current_frame.ascii` only for a small specific region; never print full boards.\nFor the most recent change, compare `previous_frame` to `current_frame`, or `last_transition.before_frame` to `last_transition.after_frame`; `history[-1].frame` is the current frame, not the previous one.\nUse Python to inspect the evidence, refine that world model from the newest history, and se